From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "adnan.drina" <adnan.drina@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:03:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/19] feat(guardrails): loop escalation starts on
 same-result, same-call and repeat-read

RHOAI3 v32 (spring-petclinic-rest-legacy-v32-qwen38): two Qwen 3.8 loops
never produced the identical-call note, so patch 0017 never escalated them.
t_449a35e4 run 1 repeated one `ls | wc -l; date; ps aux` poll hundreds of
times (results differed only by the clock line; 62.8M input tokens, iteration
budget exhausted). t_2f2509aa run 30 issued ~141 calls of one pipeline
varying only `tail -c N`, every result with the same output_sha256.

Two optional start triggers, read from the same loop_escalation block:

- same_result_count: that many consecutive calls to one tool that are
  near-duplicates of each other (patch 0014's measure, _near_duplicates over
  _near_duplicate_call / _near_duplicate_arg_call, at the guard's configured
  similarity) and return a byte-identical result (patch 0015's reading,
  _result_content_lines: the terminal envelope's output without the
  runtime's own notices).
- same_call_count: that many consecutive calls with identical arguments,
  whatever the results.

A third optional trigger, repeat-read (repeat_read_count within the last
repeat_read_window counted calls): that many calls each EXACTLY repeat an
earlier call since the last restart (same tool and arguments, byte-identical
result in 0015's reading). v32 t_56432803 run 43 cycled five brief sections
with identical results over three laps; consecutive calls differed, so no
consecutive trigger fired. Its restart-bound signature is the tool plus the
hash of the set of repeated (arguments, result) pairs in the window; a later
repeat-read set sharing a pair with an earlier one of the same tool counts as
that signature (a cycle re-detected at another phase is the same stall).

Pollers exempt under is_stall_guard_repeatable neither count nor break a
run; an edit, verification or typed-repair call (classify_end_call), a
background command, an evidence-changing tool or a board transition restarts
every count (and the repeat-read history), and so does every start and end
of an escalation. The executed call's raw result is now passed to the escalation (_observe_loop_escalation). A start on one of
these triggers appends a factual note to the triggering result (the count,
and that the results were byte-identical, the call unchanged, or the calls
exact repeats of earlier calls with identical results). The start
log line gains trigger=identical|same-result|same-call|repeat-read.

max_starts_per_signature bounds restarts: one stall signature (tool plus
argument hash, tool plus result hash for same-result, the repeated-pair set
for repeat-read) may start that many
escalations in a run; the next start on it is a guardrail halt through the
existing halt path with the code loop_escalation_restart_halt.

Running the card's typed repair executor (a terminal command naming
typed-repair.py) is a new escalation end condition, end reason=typed-repair
(v32 t_6d0f2b9f: the thinking turns named typed-repair.py as the first
action, then spent all 8 turns reading files). Like an edit or verification
call it also restarts every count. The edit and
verification patterns are unchanged.

Absent keys mean the 0017 behaviour (identical-call trigger only, unbounded).
The halting rules of 0001, 0012, 0014 and 0015, the escalation's edit and
verification end conditions, turn cap and request body are unchanged.

Tests replay the runs' kanban exec journal rows (tests/fixtures/
loop_escalation): same-call fires on the fifth unchanged poll (16:34:57Z),
same-result on the third `tail -c` call with an identical result;
repeat-read at terminal call 31 of t_56432803 run 43; same-result on run 42
nine calls before its near_duplicate_loop_halt; the restart
bound halts the third start; the Qwen 3.6 M1 capture sequence, process
polls, near-duplicates with differing results, patch 0015's paging cases
and re-reads after an edit do not fire. Local extension.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
