 """Per-worker loop escalation to a thinking request profile.
 
-When the identical-call stall guard emits its notice for the THIRD
-consecutive identical call (``STALL_GUARD_IDENTICAL_CALL_THRESHOLD``), the
-worker's following model requests use an escalation request body taken from
-the active provider's config instead of the normal one. Escalation ends at
-the first of:
+An escalation starts on one of four triggers (the ``trigger=`` field of the
+start log line):
+
+* ``identical``: the identical-call stall guard emits its notice for the
+  THIRD consecutive identical call (``STALL_GUARD_IDENTICAL_CALL_THRESHOLD``:
+  same tool, same arguments, same result);
+* ``same-result`` (``same_result_count``, optional): that many consecutive
+  calls to one tool that are near-duplicates of each other (the similarity of
+  the near-duplicate guard, patch 0014) and return a byte-identical result
+  (read as that guard reads it, patch 0015: the terminal envelope's output,
+  without the runtime's own appended notices);
+* ``same-call`` (``same_call_count``, optional): that many consecutive calls
+  with identical arguments, whatever their results;
+* ``repeat-read`` (``repeat_read_count`` within the last
+  ``repeat_read_window`` counted calls, optional): that many calls each
+  EXACTLY repeat an earlier call since the last restart (same tool, same
+  arguments, byte-identical result in the same reading). Covers a cycle of
+  different reads whose results never change, which none of the consecutive
+  triggers sees.
+
+The optional triggers never count pollers exempt from the identical-call
+notice (``is_stall_guard_repeatable``: they neither count nor break a run),
+and an edit, verification or typed-repair call (``classify_end_call``), a
+background command or another evidence-changing call restarts every count,
+including the repeat-read history (a read after an edit is never a repeat).
+Every escalation start and end restarts them too. When one of them starts an
+escalation a factual note is appended to the triggering result.
+
+``max_starts_per_signature`` (optional) bounds how many escalations one
+stall signature may start in a run; the next start on that signature is a
+guardrail halt (``loop_escalation_restart_halt``) instead. Signatures: tool
+plus argument hash (``identical``, ``same-call``), tool plus result hash
+(``same-result``), and for ``repeat-read`` tool plus the hash of the set of
+repeated (arguments, result) pairs in the window. A later repeat-read whose
+set shares a pair with an earlier repeat-read set of the same tool counts as
+that earlier signature: a cycle re-detected at another phase is the same
+stall.
+
+Once started, the worker's following model requests use an escalation
+request body taken from the active provider's config instead of the normal
+one. Escalation ends at the first of:
 
 * an edit: a ``write_file`` / ``patch`` call, or a ``terminal`` command that
   writes (``sed -i``, ``> src/``, ``tee src/``);
 * a verification: a ``terminal`` command naming ``run-verify.sh`` or
   ``advance.py``;
+* a typed repair: a ``terminal`` command naming ``typed-repair.py`` (the
+  card's typed repair executor);
 * ``max_turns`` escalated model requests (the cap).
 
 After any of these the normal body is sent again. The guardrails are not
